WEBVTT

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:22.770
<v Speaker 1>Hey, this is Tim Hyland. I'm here at the Magic the Gathering World Championships 2004 here in San Francisco, and I'm standing with Justin, Gary. Justin is a pro player who is on the national team at the championships last year. And Justin, can you give us a little bit of a perspective of what these guys, what's happening for these guys today?</v>

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:30.720
<v Speaker 2>Well, the World Championships, more than any other Magic tournament, is as much a contest of stamina as it is of skill. I mean, this is the third day they played 12 rounds of competition</v>

00:00:30.800 --> 00:00:45.440
<v Speaker 2>party in two different formats. Now they've got to prepare for 1/3 format and be ready to go to try to make the top eight. It can be really, really tough and you can see that in the players. A lot of them are dragging. It's, you know, it's really hard to keep your energy up and keep your focus to beat the best players in the world.</v>

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:49.300
<v Speaker 1>Right. And tell us what? What makes day three such a big challenge?</v>

00:00:49.410 --> 00:01:01.150
<v Speaker 2>Well, it's mirrored and it's, it's block constructed this year and a lot of people are complaining about it because I mean it's a very defined format. Affinity is everywhere. There's about over 40% of the field is Affinity and most of the top</v>

00:01:01.220 --> 00:01:19.440
<v Speaker 2>players are playing it. There's some people out there that are trying to build foiled decks just to take them down. I've seen a lot of Tooth and Nail. I've seen a lot of like green, red, just pure artifact hate. But it's a really tough field to punch through when you're playing tons of mirror matches with an Affinity deck or you're playing with pretty much everyone admits is a subpar deck. Trying to gun for the top deck, it can be really tough.</v>

00:01:19.490 --> 00:01:21.900
<v Speaker 1>And now these guys, are they tired on day three?</v>

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:42.090
<v Speaker 2>A lot of them are, I mean, you know, you're not getting that much sleep, especially after the draft day. It's particularly long. I mean, you basically focus on pure willpower to stay, to keep going. You know, you're in the rounds. You've got to get there. If you're at the top of the standings and, you know, I only need three more wins to make top eight, I can maybe be the world champion. You know, it keeps you jittery, but it's really, really hard to keep your energy.</v>

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:43.410
<v Speaker 1>Up. Wow, it sounds grueling.</v>

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:51.530
<v Speaker 2>Yeah, yeah, definitely can be. I mean, it's a, it's a, it's a really fun and amazing experience, but you gotta be tough. You gotta, you gotta be prepared for it to be a world champion.</v>

00:01:51.740 --> 00:01:52.370
<v Speaker 1>So</v>

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:55.360
<v Speaker 1>what are the races that we should keep our eyes on right now, today?</v>

00:01:55.430 --> 00:02:22.800
<v Speaker 2>Well, I've been following three things. The main things you want to be looking for are who's gonna be making the top 8. Camille Cornelsen still standing tall at only one loss. AO Piquette and Manual Bavand are still at 2 losses and very strong. One of the other two losses, Antoine Ruel actually did something amazing I have never seen before from a player in contention. He sat down this morning with his Meriden block deck. He didn't like it too much, so he traded decks with another French player across from him,</v>

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:53.850
<v Speaker 2>having never seen the deck before. He just traded decks, crossed off the name and wrote his own name down and now he's playing for top eight with a deck that he just picked up from some guy today. I don't believe it. We're gonna it's gonna be amazing if he makes it. He could be a genius. I don't know the other two stories that you really want to be looking for the national team competition. It has never, ever been this close before. We have the Japan as currently in the lead at the end of round one with 75 points, followed by a close tie with South Korea and Belgium at 72 points. Ukraine, who was in the lead at the end of day</v>

00:02:53.920 --> 00:03:15.500
<v Speaker 2>one when O3 in the first round today and now they've fallen back to, you know, 13th place or so. But everybody is close. There's tons of teams within one round difference of coming of coming in to be the make it to the finals. The last thing you want to look at is the player of the year race. We were talking with Kai Budde today. This is the first time that he's no longer in contention for Player of the Year in the last three years.</v>

00:03:15.550 --> 00:03:16.300
<v Speaker 1>So tell us who?</v>

00:03:16.310 --> 00:03:23.920
<v Speaker 2>Is the people in contention? Nikolai Herzog has the edge. He was the number one coming into this weekend. Unfortunately hasn't put in an amazing performance.</v>

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:54.250
<v Speaker 2>He's at 76 going going into this round. It's a it's a very disappointing day for him yesterday when draft, which is his strong format. Gabriel Nassif, however, in second place is looking really strong. He's 11 and two at this point and he's on the French national team, which means he's going to get extra pro tour points for playing on on on Saturday. If the French team wins, he won't even need to make top 8 to take this title. But don't knock out there's plenty of other people in contention. Richard Osterberg has 93 and 1. He can make it with the top 8 performance. So it's a really close tight</v>

00:03:54.330 --> 00:03:58.050
<v Speaker 2>based on all of these levels, it should be the one of the most exciting worlds I've seen in a while.</v>

00:03:58.220 --> 00:04:02.190
<v Speaker 1>And Justin, do you have any predictions yourself of who you think might win?</v>

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:10.780
<v Speaker 2>Well, it's tough, I gotta say. Nasif seems unbeatable today and if he keeps up this kind of record, I think I'd have to give it to him for that race.</v>

00:04:11.460 --> 00:04:21.360
<v Speaker 1>Wow. Right on. All right. Well, it sounds really, really exciting and we're really excited to be here at the World Championships of Magic the Gathering 2004 here in San Francisco. We'll be back with more later. Thanks.</v>

